galaxy-rule-c371a8a6.symmetry_origin:
The Galaxy Rule is not derived from fundamental symmetry principles in the same way as conservation laws. It is an empirical relation that emerges from the interplay of gravity, baryonic matter distribution, and stellar evolution. However, it is invariant under certain transformations relevant to observational astronomy, such as changes in viewing angle (as long as the galaxy is not edge-on, which would affect the direct measurement of 


V_{rot}

), and it reflects the underlying physical processes governing the formation and evolution of galaxies within the standard cosmological model.

galaxy-rule-c371a8a6.conceptual_definition:
The Galaxy Rule, also known as the Baryonic Tully-Fisher relation, establishes an empirical correlation between the luminosity (
L
) of a spiral galaxy and its rotational velocity (


V_{rot}

). It posits that more luminous galaxies tend to rotate faster, and this relationship is generally found to follow a power law with an exponent 

α

typically between 3 and 4.
